Thank you.

00:01:57.343 --> 00:01:59.830
- Well, we are honored to be of service.

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.730
And for everyone listening,

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.400
we're gonna be taking questions

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.400
for Jonathan and myself
if you're interested.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.280
So please put the comments
on YouTube or Facebook,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:09.113
wherever you're watching.

00:02:09.113 --> 00:02:10.830
And we have team members
who will surface it.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:15.830
But Jonathan, you have a
fascinating job area of research.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.620
You are a social psychologist

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:22.420
focused on the psychology of morality.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:23.500
What is that?

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.410
And that'll help people start
thinking of questions, too.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:26.850
- Sure, okay.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.010
Well, so morality refers to the fact that

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.040
we're always judging each other.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:32.990
We don't just live in a physical world.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:37.640
We live in a world made
of norms and expectations.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.150
And those keep us in line,

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.030
they give us endless things
to talk about, gossip about

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.940
they lead to conflict, endless conflict,

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.330
but morality also makes
possible cooperation.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:51.400
We're an amazingly cooperative species.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.860
Bees are more cooperative than us,

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.920
but not a lot of other creatures.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:59.290
So cooperation and conflict,
that's a lot of what I study.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:02.040
And that has brought me
into the study of politics,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.040
in particular.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:06.570
I used to look at how morality
varied across cultures

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.580
and beginning as the
cultural war began to heat up

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.490
in the United States in
the 1990s and early 2000s,

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:13.990
I began to look at how left and right

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:15.733
are like different countries.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:18.871
- Yeah, I definitely
want to talk about that.

00:03:18.871 --> 00:03:21.380
I'm sure there's gonna
be questions about it.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:25.540
When you look at your three
publications, kind of common,

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.470
every day I read a publications
outside of academics,

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.530
you have three books.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.560
One is The Happiness Hypothesis:

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.370
Finding Modern Truth in Ancient Wisdom.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.890
I'd love your big takeaways there.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.800
And I am curious how
it's affected your life.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:40.640
Your other one is

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:41.530
The Righteous Mind:

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:44.120
Why Good People Are Divided
by Politics and Religion.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:45.430
And then most recently

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:46.960
The Coddling of the American Mind:

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.170
How Good Intentions and Bad Ideas

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.670
Are Setting Up a Generation for Failure.

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:53.630
And so that'll also help prompt people

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:55.770
to think about what questions to ask.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:58.150
But I mean, let's just go in order

00:03:58.150 --> 00:03:59.310
from The Happiness Hypothesis,

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.600
this is something I spent a
lot of time thinking about,

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.960
about how to optimize not only my own life

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.290
but how to optimize generally society

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:08.750
so that we can be happier.

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:10.100
What are kind of your takeaways?

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:11.620
I mean, how has it
affected your personal life

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.580
when you did this research
to look at each with them?

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:15.413
- Sure.

00:04:15.413 --> 00:04:16.920
So what the book is,

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.830
it grows out of my course at
the University of Virginia.

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:20.670
I taught there for 17 years,

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.770
and I would try to present

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.500
psychological insights to students.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:28.030
And I would often use quotes
from the ancient East and West.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:30.640
And I found that there's
a small number of ideas

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.163
that have been discovered
in almost every society.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.217
Like, "There's nothing good or bad,

00:04:35.217 --> 00:04:38.850
"but thinking makes it
so," as Shakespeare said.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:41.270
And that's the basis of cognitive therapy.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:44.920
So I wrote a book one
truth in each chapter,

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.110
and I reviewed what's the
modern research on it.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:49.730
And the book wasn't supposed
to be about happiness,

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:53.550
but it turns out that if you
take a lot of ancient wisdom,

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.490
there are a lot of connecting threads

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:58.170
on how to live a good
life, how to flourish,

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.460
and I'll save you all the
trouble of buying the book.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.290
I'll summarize thousands of
years in a couple of sentences.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:05.140
(Sal laughs)

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:06.460
What it comes down to,

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:09.180
is happiness doesn't come
from getting what you want.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.440
Okay, that's very short, lived.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:14.630
Happiness comes from within
more, so that's better.

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:18.260
But really the best way to
summarize the human condition

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:20.850
is that happiness comes from between.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:22.120
That is...

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:22.953
Pardon me.

00:05:22.953 --> 00:05:25.900
It comes from getting the
right kinds of relationships

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.000
between yourself and others,

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:29.580
yourself and your work,

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:31.350
some sort of productive work

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:34.060
and yourself and something
larger than yourself,

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:35.450
having a sense that your life matters.

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:36.890
You're part of something bigger.

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:38.870
And if you get those
three conditions right,

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:41.860
you're gonna be about
as happy as you can be.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:43.520
So we all have a kind of a set point,

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.060
a general level of
happiness that we return to.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:48.750
But if you get those
three conditions right,

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.530
you'll sort of be generally above that

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:52.400
at the high end of that range.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:53.900
So there you go.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:54.918
The secrets of happiness.

00:05:54.918 --> 00:05:57.720
Trust me, it's a lot more
interesting when you go it slowly.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.280
I shouldn't just cut to the
chase and given you the answer.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:03.170
- No, well, I think I have the book,

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:05.380
and then I encourage
everyone to go through it.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.380
But there's a lot that I talk a lot about

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:11.410
on this about my own journeys
and meditation, but I do.

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:13.770
And we also talk about how
important it is to have a purpose

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:16.480
and feel like you're
contributing to something larger.

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:20.320
And so that definitely
reinforces my existing beliefs.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.850
And as you also write
about confirmation bias,

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:24.054
it's a very (laughs) (indistinct)-

00:06:24.054 --> 00:06:26.097
- And just to make that
connection that's right.

00:06:26.097 --> 00:06:29.440
It always feels good to be proven right.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:30.998
And it's often hard to prove people wrong.

00:06:30.998 --> 00:06:32.189
- (laughs) That's right.

00:06:32.189 --> 00:06:33.022
And then related to that,

00:06:33.022 --> 00:06:34.680
I think that gets into

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.570
a lot of what you've
been focused on lately,

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:38.330
including with your not-for-profit,

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:42.130
which is, it's clear that
there's significant polarization

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:45.510
politics is by definition,
always somewhat polarizing.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:46.590
But there is a scent,

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:48.580
and you probably have evidence

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:51.200
that it is unusually polarized right now.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.880
I'd love if you have any
evidence that it is truly,

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:56.000
it's not just an intuitive sense

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.680
that it is measurably
unusually polarized right now.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.620
And also, what do you think

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:03.730
are the sociological cognitive reasons?

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:06.477
And then maybe we could go
into how do we depolarize

00:07:08.067 --> 00:07:09.733
or if we think we should.
- Sure, Okay.

00:07:09.733 --> 00:07:13.310
So as you said, politics
is by nature polarizing.

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:16.120
You have parties that
represent groups of interests

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:17.950
or identities or whatever,

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:20.640
and then they compete, and
elections are zero-sum.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.300
So there's nothing wrong with that.

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.140
And even some degree of polarization,

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.930
like peoples, are far apart on views.

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:27.333
It's not necessarily bad.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:29.310
But think of it this way,

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:31.130
suppose you have a sports league

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:33.640
in which the Yankees and
the Red Sox hate each other,

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:34.803
just theoretically.

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:37.660
That can actually make the game more fun,

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:40.730
but suppose they hate each other so much

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:42.900
that they start hiring hitmen

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.260
to kill members of the other team.

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:48.030
And they do things to sabotage the field.

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:50.160
Okay, now you're like,
you're totally out of bounds.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.420
This is not what the game is.

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:54.240
Now let's turn to American politics.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:57.400
So there's some debate
among political scientists

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:58.740
about what polarization means.

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:00.010
And if you think about like

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:03.790
views on on policy issues like abortion,

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:06.570
by some niches, we're not
more polarized than we were.

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:09.830
As a psychologist, I focus on
one thing and one thing alone.

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:13.470
That is an effective polarization,
which means emotional,

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:15.480
how much do you feel emotion, negative?

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:17.510
How much do you hate the other side?

00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:19.140
That's what's crucial.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:20.930
And the data shows very clearly

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:24.260
that in the '70s and '80s,
and even into the '90s,

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:25.520
Republicans, and Democrats,

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:27.027
they rated the people on their side

00:08:27.027 --> 00:08:28.640
and the parties on the other side,

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:32.323
sort of little a little
negative, but not at the bottom.

00:08:32.323 --> 00:08:35.440
And the ratings began to
get extremely negative

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:36.800
in the late '90s is when it begins.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.940
And then it goes down and down and down.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:40.900
There is such hatred now

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:43.530
that this has a lot of ramifications.

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:45.230
One of which is

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:48.790
we are now really willing
to believe almost anything.

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:49.920
If you hate the other side,

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.960
you're much more willing to
believe crazy stuff about them.

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:55.580
So that's one whole piece
is rising polarization.

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:56.880
And then let's add in

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:00.160
the change in social
media and in cable TV.

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:02.400
So we now have a media ecosystem

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:04.270
that is really well designed

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:06.440
to find the most anger-provoking,

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:09.222
outrageous stuff it can be totalized,

00:09:09.222 --> 00:09:10.720
but it's gonna funnel it in.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:14.770
And so for a lot of reasons
where now we're in a democracy,

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:16.460
which has to have a public square

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:18.570
in which ideas are hashed out.

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:20.080
And we generally make progress.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:23.300
We improve ideas when
sometimes there's compromise,

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:25.990
I mean, that's what we need
to do to live in a democracy.

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:28.040
And I think it's much
harder to do that now

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:29.390
than it was even 10 years ago

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:31.240
to say nothing of 20 or 30 years ago.

00:09:32.118 --> 00:09:33.830
- And what do you think it was in the '90s

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:36.510
and then it sounds like it
accelerated in the last 20 years

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:38.253
that caused this?

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:42.310
I guess you could say
amplification of polarization

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:44.550
or judgment of other side.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.110
- This could be mess, yeah.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:50.390
So, our minds like to look
for a thing that happened,

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:51.223
'cause you see the curves,

00:09:51.223 --> 00:09:54.440
and they really do begin
to dip in the '90s,

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:55.273
and you might say,

00:09:55.273 --> 00:09:58.557
"Oh, well, it must've been,
Newt Gingrich or Fox News."

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:01.950
As you thought you're safe
if you're on the left,

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:02.820
you'd certainly point to that.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:03.653
If you're on the right,

00:10:03.653 --> 00:10:05.403
I imagine you'd point to Bill Clinton.

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:08.870
But what I like to do is

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:10.520
these are complex systems

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:12.227
that have a lot of parameters to them.

00:10:12.227 --> 00:10:13.890
And I like to think of what are the forces

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.220
that are kind of pulling
any complex system together?

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:18.350
What are the forces that
are blowing it apart?

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:22.360
And a lot of them began
changing around this time.

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:23.240
So for one thing,

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:25.420
we have to see the late 20th century

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:27.350
as an historical anomaly

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:31.100
when our democracy had lots of problems.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:35.240
But we thought democracy
was the ultimately

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:36.880
the way that everyone was gonna live,

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:38.617
once the Soviet Union
imploded, we thought,

00:10:38.617 --> 00:10:42.057
"Wow, liberal democracy
with free-market capitalism,

00:10:42.057 --> 00:10:45.800
"this is the end of history
everyone's gonna go this way."

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:46.960
What we didn't realize is that

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:49.400
democracy is actually really, really hard,

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:52.070
and the founding fathers
and the Federalist Papers,

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:54.850
they really worried about
our tendency to faction,

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:58.380
to split apart and hate the other side.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:10:59.760
So I'll just point to a couple of factors.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:01.530
One is the loss of a common enemy.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.930
So from Pearl Harbor and world
war II, through the Cold War,

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.750
having a common enemy really
binds a group together.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:08.663
That's a big one.

00:11:09.590 --> 00:11:12.770
Another really big one
is the media environment.

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:13.880
After World War II,

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:15.710
we had three television networks

00:11:15.710 --> 00:11:16.870
that had a fairness doctrine.

00:11:16.870 --> 00:11:17.840
So they had...

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:19.900
They couldn't be very political.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:21.750
So for awhile, we had a media system

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:24.520
in which we kind of all
got the same sort of news.

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:26.280
And then once you get cable TV,

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:27.260
and then the internet,

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:29.010
and then social media,

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:31.660
now you go from broadcasting to everyone

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:34.530
so that we all hear the
same stuff to narrowcasting.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:35.363
And of course,

00:11:35.363 --> 00:11:37.633
people using Facebook
data and advertising data

00:11:37.633 --> 00:11:39.760
that really micro-target.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:41.210
So we're not on the same page.

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:42.410
We don't believe the same things.

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:45.780
We're in a state of moral
and factual incoherence.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:48.130
And we may be that way
for the rest of our lives.

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:49.790
So those are a couple of big ones,

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:52.370
but there are many others
that began changing,

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:55.487
and the result is hatred,
distrust, distrust of each other,

00:11:55.487 --> 00:11:58.790
and distrust of institutions
has been rising steadily

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:02.160
to the point where I
think extremely dangerous.

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:05.560
It's really not clear what's
gonna happen to our country.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:07.960
- I know you're out in the
business of predicting,

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:10.360
but what do you think is...

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:12.930
What do you think is a
natural extrapolation

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:13.900
from where we are right now?

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:15.147
If you go out five or 10 years.

00:12:15.147 --> 00:12:16.313
- Oh boy.

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:19.330
Well, put it this way.

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:21.907
If current trends continue,

00:12:21.907 --> 00:12:24.850
then our country would probably fall apart

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:26.020
in a catastrophic way.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:27.140
But please don't quote me,

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:28.220
I know people are watching live.

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:29.730
Please don't me-
- Well, that is the statement

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:30.728
that I was expecting you to say.

00:12:30.728 --> 00:12:33.360
- Wait, I got to finish.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:34.193
I gotta finish it,

00:12:34.193 --> 00:12:36.750
because if current trends continue,

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:38.740
then I think we will be in big trouble.

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:40.930
But the thing is, current
trends never continue.

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:42.470
This certainly not continue forever.

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:43.930
And if you zoom out,

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:45.420
if you take the big picture

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:49.260
and you look at both human history

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:51.500
in which life is getting better,

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:53.603
rights are being extended.

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:56.950
Science advances, knowledge...

00:12:56.950 --> 00:12:58.890
The big picture life is
getting better and better.

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:02.650
And then there are dips,
but then it keeps rising.

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:03.880
Steve Pinker has books like

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:06.390
The Better Angels of Our Nature.

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:07.320
There are a variety of books

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:09.500
that have really tried to
encourage us to realize

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:11.480
in every age, people
think things are terrible,

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:12.313
and they're going to hell,

00:13:12.313 --> 00:13:14.900
but actually, things are
generally getting better.

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:17.700
So it's always been wrong
to bet against America.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:21.680
And I think in China and
a few other countries,

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:25.610
they are enjoying our mess
for geostrategic reasons.

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:28.590
Odds are, we'll figure a way out of this.

00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:30.748
So my short answer is

00:13:30.748 --> 00:13:35.748
I can't predict I think
there're variety of scenarios

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:38.810
in which things look
even scarier and weirder

00:13:40.610 --> 00:13:42.497
next year and next year or two.

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:46.490
But we're likely to find ways out of this.

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:47.610
We have to solve a lot of problems.

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:51.647
We've got to improve so many
things about our democracy.

00:13:51.647 --> 00:13:53.599
The way we vote,

00:13:53.599 --> 00:13:57.720
the sense that people have
a voice, gerrymandering.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.510
There's all these problems
with our democracy.

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:05.180
And I do hope that if things
change politically next year,

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:06.960
that democracy reform and improvement

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:08.643
will be very high on the agenda.

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:11.880
- Yeah, and you talk about the importance,

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:13.529
I guess, of a common enemy

00:14:13.529 --> 00:14:16.470
to hold people a little
bit closer together.

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:18.170
And obviously, we are facing

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:20.550
something of a common
enemy in the pandemic.

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:22.610
And from YouTube, Sutton, asks,

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:24.050
do you think...

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:25.860
Well, this is more of
epidemiological question.

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:27.030
Do you think it will have a second wave,

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:28.590
and it sounds like it already is, frankly,

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:30.830
but if we do, how do you
think it'll affect society?

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:32.180
Will it cause Democrats and Republicans

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:33.013
to have a greater divide,

00:14:33.013 --> 00:14:35.623
or will they finally come together?

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:38.310
- Yeah, so let's back up and go back

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:40.960
to those healthy on early
days of the pandemic

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:42.780
when we were all trying to come to grips.

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:45.240
It was inconceivable that we'd
have to shut everything down

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:47.950
and stay home for months, inconceivable.

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:49.200
And as we came to grips with it,

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:50.800
and as that happened,

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:54.537
it like, "Oh my God,
everyone is doing this.

00:14:54.537 --> 00:14:56.420
"This is a global thing."

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:57.660
And in those first weeks,

00:14:57.660 --> 00:14:59.197
that was the big question
people were asking,

00:14:59.197 --> 00:15:01.120
"Is this gonna bring us together?"

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:03.680
But let's step back here

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:05.830
and understand what happens to societies

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:09.600
when you have a foreign attack
that brings people together

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:12.190
when you have a pandemic in history,

00:15:12.190 --> 00:15:14.030
that divides people apart.

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:15.240
You may have a disease coming in.

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:16.330
People are afraid of each other.

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:17.210
There are shortages.

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:20.320
So pandemics traditionally have
actually split people apart.

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:21.910
Now this pandemic is different

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:24.060
because we didn't have food shortages.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:26.000
It wasn't that deadly like the plague.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:28.490
I mean, it wasn't like, half
the people were gonna die.

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:31.520
And it was an amazing shared experience.

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:32.640
So in the early days,

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:35.937
the polling showed many
more people were saying,

00:15:35.937 --> 00:15:37.820
"We're all in this together."

00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:40.110
But here's the amazing and messed up

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.210
and tragic thing about our country.

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:45.010
Our culture war is so severe

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:48.600
that within, it took about four
or five weeks by mid-April,

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:51.620
the pandemic itself had
divided the country.

00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:53.600
Now, I think a lot of
that can be laid directly

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:55.787
at the foot of the
president who was tweeting,

00:15:55.787 --> 00:15:58.710
"Liberate Virginia," things like that.

00:15:58.710 --> 00:16:03.710
So both the president,
the media environment,

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:05.850
within a few weeks,

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:08.300
we had to left over to wearing masks,

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:09.850
over the speed of opening up,

00:16:09.850 --> 00:16:12.440
over so many other things.

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:14.910
So I don't think that the pandemic

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:17.900
will help or bring us
together at a national level.

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:19.640
Now, locally, lots of great stuff happens.

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:22.160
So you got to look at multiple levels,

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:24.720
but overall America has
handled this pandemic

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:27.730
horrifically, incompetently,
and of course,

00:16:27.730 --> 00:16:29.920
you can blame many actors and agencies,

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:32.510
but a portion of the
story is our polarization.

00:16:32.510 --> 00:16:34.923
We don't trust each other or authorities.

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:37.878
- Yeah, and it related
to this, or maybe not,

00:16:37.878 --> 00:16:38.758
I mean, you tell me.

00:16:38.758 --> 00:16:40.000
Your most recent book,

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:42.340
The Coddling of the American Mind,

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:45.190
has this played into this polarization?

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:46.910
Has polarization played into that?

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:50.277
Or is that just another
orthogonal dimension

00:16:50.277 --> 00:16:52.408
to what's going on in society?

00:16:52.408 --> 00:16:53.983
- Yeah, so briefly,

00:16:53.983 --> 00:16:55.410
The Coddling of the American Mind

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:57.120
is about the big changes

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:00.370
that we began to see on
campus and in high schools,

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:01.710
as Gen Z came in.

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:05.020
Gen Z, people born in 1996 or 1997,

00:17:05.020 --> 00:17:06.150
depending on how you count it.

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:07.443
But around that mid-90s.

00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:11.120
Their childhoods are different
from the millennials,

00:17:11.120 --> 00:17:13.400
and their mental health
profiles are different.

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:16.170
They have much higher rates
of anxiety and depression,

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:18.800
nothing else, really just
anxiety and depression

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:19.732
and is especially-
- How much high?

00:17:19.732 --> 00:17:20.907
Is that well documented, the rate?

00:17:20.907 --> 00:17:23.220
- Oh yes, it's very well documented, yes.

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:27.700
If you go to the coddling.com
somewhere on the front page,

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:29.920
I have a link to a giant Google Doc

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:33.670
where I've collected all
those studies showing

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:36.890
on any side, is it rising?

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:38.290
And then a second document

00:17:38.290 --> 00:17:40.860
is social media part of the cause.

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:42.940
And when we started this with this...

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:45.490
Was originally, the insight
was from Greg Lukianoff

00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:50.040
when we started this
with Lukianoff on, 2015,

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:52.710
everyone on campuses
were across the country

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:55.780
talking about the rise
of mental health needs.

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:57.860
But we didn't have any hard data on it.

00:17:57.860 --> 00:17:59.700
And by 2016, 2017,

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:03.030
now all the survey data
was showing this is real.

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:04.090
And it's not just self-report.

00:18:04.090 --> 00:18:06.640
It's not just that Gen Z is
comfortable talking about it

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:09.660
because you find the same
increase for self-harm

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:12.680
and you find a similar
increase on suicide.

00:18:12.680 --> 00:18:13.630
So this is behavior,

00:18:13.630 --> 00:18:16.440
it's not just changes
in diagnostic criteria.

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:18.000
So we're trying to understand why that is.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:19.460
And I decided a portion of it,

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:20.970
especially the sex difference

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:23.120
may be, or seems to be doing part

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:25.020
to the effect of social media,

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:26.620
especially on middle school kids.

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:28.907
Social media really
changes around 2011, 2012,

00:18:28.907 --> 00:18:31.580
in that for 2009 to 2012.

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:34.410
But the other piece is
the vast overprotection

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:35.920
that Americans put on kids.

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:38.270
And it happened in Canada and Britain too.

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:40.490
We stopped letting them
out to play as much.

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:43.160
We got really scared about abduction,

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:44.587
and we overprotected kids.

00:18:44.587 --> 00:18:47.530
Kids most need to grow
socially as free play

00:18:47.530 --> 00:18:48.540
and supervised free play,

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:49.510
where they get in conflicts,

00:18:49.510 --> 00:18:51.670
they work things out themselves.

00:18:51.670 --> 00:18:54.286
So that's what that book is about.

00:18:54.286 --> 00:18:55.330
No, I don't think

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:58.053
it doesn't tell us much
about the pandemic,

00:18:59.800 --> 00:19:01.950
but yes, we should
definitely talk about it

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:04.370
as an educational nonprofit.

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:05.600
I don't know, actually

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:07.250
Sal, are you seeing any signs of this?

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:12.250
I mean, your audience is
mostly, I would guess, Gen Z.

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:15.110
Are you seeing signs
that Gen Z is different

00:19:15.110 --> 00:19:18.163
from millennials or old
previous generations?

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:20.730
- Well, the very nature of Khan Academy,

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:22.900
we are removed physically.

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:26.790
So we're only engaging in
kind of through the exercises

00:19:26.790 --> 00:19:29.000
with the videos that they might watch.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:30.670
And so we don't see it as directly

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:33.360
there's other kind of
non-Khan Academy outlets

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:35.629
where at least, I hate to admit it,

00:19:35.629 --> 00:19:37.110
but as a middle-aged person.

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:39.295
I'm like, "Oh, these youngsters
now they are so different."

00:19:39.295 --> 00:19:41.545
- Welcome to the club, yes.

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:43.850
- And I wonder whether

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:47.470
this is just something that
every generation goes through,

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:49.350
once all of a sudden you turn 40.

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:50.810
And all of a sudden,

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:52.671
you think you're not that
different than a 25-year-old,

00:19:52.671 --> 00:19:55.090
but you realize that cultural difference.

00:19:55.090 --> 00:19:57.901
Or maybe they're just
like I was when I was 25.

00:19:57.901 --> 00:19:59.506
(laughs) I'm different now when I'm 40.

00:19:59.506 --> 00:20:01.235
- Yeah, no.

00:20:01.235 --> 00:20:03.250
It's true that each generation

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:07.060
thinks the one behind it is lazy or...

00:20:07.060 --> 00:20:08.970
Every generation criticizes
the one behind it.

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:10.450
And often the criticisms are the same

00:20:10.450 --> 00:20:11.750
across thousands of years.

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:13.313
So you're right to wonder that.

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:16.460
But if you look at the mental health data,

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:19.650
something really happened around 2012

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:22.550
is when the lines began
going way, way up for girls

00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:25.750
and up, not as much up,
but still also for boys.

00:20:25.750 --> 00:20:28.210
So these are objective
measures of mental health

00:20:28.210 --> 00:20:30.280
in terms of those that are behavioral.

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:31.113
So it is.

00:20:31.113 --> 00:20:32.760
And the other thing I can say is,

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:34.220
I've spoken about the book

00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:37.920
at many high schools and
universities to Gen Z audiences.

00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:40.410
And I laid out the case.

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:42.750
Here's what we think is happening.

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:45.200
And I was asked them, do
you think this is right?

00:20:46.588 --> 00:20:48.163
Or, I said something,

00:20:48.163 --> 00:20:50.500
painting your generation a certain way.

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:54.210
And overwhelmingly, they
say, it's a correct portrait

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:57.869
because they recognize the
effects of overprotection

00:20:57.869 --> 00:21:01.070
and of being on social media so heavily,

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:02.793
Gen Z is not in denial.

00:21:04.540 --> 00:21:05.978
What I've seen is they generally

00:21:05.978 --> 00:21:08.420
want a broader range of experience.

00:21:08.420 --> 00:21:10.640
They wanna be exposed to ideas.

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:13.313
They wanna grow up to be
stronger and successful.

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:16.760
- And so, you have these two factors,

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:18.344
the social to social
media fails to understand

00:21:18.344 --> 00:21:19.177
that these would have been kids

00:21:19.177 --> 00:21:24.177
who are essentially entering
adolescents around 2012, 2013,

00:21:24.260 --> 00:21:25.880
which is right when, obviously,

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:28.090
I mean, social media had already
started to gain momentum,

00:21:28.090 --> 00:21:29.660
but around 2012, 2013,

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:32.211
it became huge, your
Facebook, your Instagram,

00:21:32.211 --> 00:21:35.080
Snapchat of the world.

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:38.161
And what do you think is this,

00:21:38.161 --> 00:21:39.980
how does social media connect?

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:42.310
And there's a question
if I can still find it

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:43.630
where someone was asking,

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:45.130
I think it's off of YouTube,

00:21:45.130 --> 00:21:49.700
do you think that is it a net
negative that social media

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:54.700
or are there kind of
from bouncing influences?

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:58.080
- Sure, so yeah.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:21:59.780
The important thing that
people need to understand

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:01.780
about social media

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:04.610
is that it really changed in two years.

00:22:04.610 --> 00:22:05.850
So it comes out, you get,

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:09.560
Myspace and friends joining
Facebook around 2004,

00:22:09.560 --> 00:22:12.857
and it's, "Here I am, here's my picture.

00:22:12.857 --> 00:22:14.087
"Here are the bands I like.

00:22:14.087 --> 00:22:16.790
"Here are my links to my various friends."

00:22:16.790 --> 00:22:18.167
That's not toxic.

00:22:18.167 --> 00:22:20.770
That's not bad for anyone's mental health.

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:24.610
In 2009, Facebook adds the Like button

00:22:24.610 --> 00:22:26.420
and Twitter add the Retweet button.

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:29.830
And now there's a huge amount
of information on engagement.

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:33.090
And everybody is
reinforcing everybody else.

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:35.883
It's almost like an operant
conditioning experiment,

00:22:37.358 --> 00:22:39.820
with James...

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:43.590
Anyway, the behaviorist
conditioning experiments.

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:45.150
And so you have so much engagement data.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:48.790
Now, the platforms use that to
algorithm size but they feed.

00:22:48.790 --> 00:22:51.550
And so now social media
becomes much more addictive.

00:22:51.550 --> 00:22:53.433
It lures many more people in,

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:56.940
and it creates an outreach machine

00:22:56.940 --> 00:22:59.037
that is now it's very
easy to drop something in

00:22:59.037 --> 00:23:00.687
and it spreads.

00:23:00.687 --> 00:23:03.690
And so by 2012, the meeting
environment is different.

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:05.450
Now, the newspapers, the old line media

00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:07.260
has to play the game.

00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:09.340
Advertising shifts onto Facebook,

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:12.240
by 2014 this thing is so powerful

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:14.500
that the Russians who've been
messing with our democracy

00:23:14.500 --> 00:23:16.740
since the mid 20th century,

00:23:16.740 --> 00:23:18.950
the Russians activate all their networks

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:21.510
to make us even more angry at each other

00:23:21.510 --> 00:23:24.250
and put in a lot of lies
and a lot of manipulation,

00:23:24.250 --> 00:23:26.190
a lot of fake sites.

00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:27.960
So 2014 now, it's like,

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:30.300
we've got this where our public squares,

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:31.133
this outreach machine

00:23:31.133 --> 00:23:33.460
being directly manipulated
by other countries

00:23:33.460 --> 00:23:35.580
and by bad actors within this country.

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:37.580
And so I think that's when 2014, 2015

00:23:37.580 --> 00:23:40.900
is when our politics really
felt like it was going haywire.

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:42.250
And this, I believe,

00:23:42.250 --> 00:23:45.160
is made possible and
opening for Donald Trump.

00:23:45.160 --> 00:23:46.600
I don't think could have been elected

00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:48.160
in a normal election year.

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:53.160
But he was very skilled
at using this free media.

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:55.580
So I just wanna make the point,

00:23:55.580 --> 00:23:56.413
people have to understand,

00:23:56.413 --> 00:23:58.509
it's not just like being on social media,

00:23:58.509 --> 00:24:03.100
it's being immeshed in this
stream of constant judgment

00:24:03.100 --> 00:24:05.760
and outrage, and it's exhausting,

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:08.140
and it's especially bad for, I believe,

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:09.390
for middle school kids.

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:11.120
So, I'm not taking a position

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:13.420
on whether adults should use it.

00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:17.110
But I think 11, 12, 13-year-olds
really should not be on it.

00:24:17.110 --> 00:24:17.950
They certainly should not be on it

00:24:17.950 --> 00:24:19.060
without the permission of their parents,

00:24:19.060 --> 00:24:21.400
but at present, anyone can
sign up for an account.

00:24:21.400 --> 00:24:23.210
There's no verification.

00:24:23.210 --> 00:24:26.080
And you see kids get sucked into this,

00:24:26.080 --> 00:24:31.017
and the chronic anxiety about
being judged about conflicts,

00:24:31.017 --> 00:24:33.310
high cortisol levels

00:24:33.310 --> 00:24:37.653
extended over, days, weeks,
months is bad for development.

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:39.530
- And this is really interesting.

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:41.630
'Cause just as an observer,

00:24:41.630 --> 00:24:42.807
I think a lot of people will say,

00:24:42.807 --> 00:24:45.117
"Oh yeah, social media,
it makes you insecure,

00:24:45.117 --> 00:24:47.687
"maybe anxious because you
see other people's best lies.

00:24:47.687 --> 00:24:48.687
"They're going on vacations.

00:24:48.687 --> 00:24:49.667
"They have perfect.

00:24:49.667 --> 00:24:51.617
"They look perfect in the
morning, et cetera, et cetera."

00:24:51.617 --> 00:24:53.890
And you feel insecure
about your very real life

00:24:53.890 --> 00:24:55.740
you're saying, and
maybe that's part of it,

00:24:55.740 --> 00:24:56.573
but you're saying,

00:24:56.573 --> 00:24:58.041
it's something a little
bit different is that

00:24:58.041 --> 00:25:01.480
it's reinforcing views in a bubble

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:06.200
and allowing to feel kind of
moral outrage about another.

00:25:06.200 --> 00:25:08.400
And the other is not even in your bubble.

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:12.810
So they're not even in a
position to defend themselves.

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:14.230
And so that causes the anxiety.

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:15.070
'Cause you're just looking at it.

00:25:15.070 --> 00:25:17.437
You're like, "I can't believe
there's half the country

00:25:17.437 --> 00:25:19.787
"or half the world that is that crazy,

00:25:19.787 --> 00:25:20.697
"that they're after me

00:25:20.697 --> 00:25:22.180
"they're after people I care about."

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:23.990
- Yeah, well, not exactly

00:25:23.990 --> 00:25:25.520
let me separate two different things.

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:28.300
There's the mental health effects

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:32.690
which seem to be especially
prominent in early adolescents

00:25:32.690 --> 00:25:34.160
and especially in girls.

00:25:34.160 --> 00:25:37.500
So when social life went
from lots of face to face,

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:39.980
gathering together with your
friends, to doing it online,

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:43.200
where there's constant judgment,
and anything can explode

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:46.070
that I think is particularly
bad for teen mental health.

00:25:46.070 --> 00:25:47.650
That's one piece of it.

00:25:47.650 --> 00:25:49.220
Then there's the democracy piece,

00:25:49.220 --> 00:25:50.843
which is not about kids.

00:25:50.843 --> 00:25:52.650
And it's not about anxiety.

00:25:52.650 --> 00:25:56.510
It's about anger and the
ability to find truth.

00:25:56.510 --> 00:25:58.580
And so that is a part of the reason

00:25:58.580 --> 00:26:00.280
I think that our democracy
is going, haywire.

00:26:00.280 --> 00:26:01.520
And why we're seeing

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:04.310
some similar movements in
many Western countries,

00:26:04.310 --> 00:26:05.580
there's all kinds of economic causes too.

00:26:05.580 --> 00:26:06.850
I don't wanna oversimplify.

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:10.170
But I was born in 1963.

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:12.110
I'm a product of the 20th century.

00:26:12.110 --> 00:26:13.690
My generation, we were raised to believe

00:26:13.690 --> 00:26:15.910
democracy is the greatest
thing, it conquered.

00:26:15.910 --> 00:26:17.760
It was better than all other systems.

00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:19.700
And if you're born in 1996,

00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:21.909
and all you've seen is the 21st century,

00:26:21.909 --> 00:26:24.830
it's not so clear that
democracy is this great thing.

00:26:24.830 --> 00:26:25.663
I'm not saying, it's not,

00:26:25.663 --> 00:26:28.820
I'm just saying it's not
working that well these days,

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:30.870
and it's not just an
American then it's happening.

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:32.970
And I think social media is a part of it.

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:36.308
It's not most of it, but
it is an accelerated,

00:26:36.308 --> 00:26:37.771
it just makes...

00:26:37.771 --> 00:26:40.120
It's exactly what the
founding fathers feared

00:26:40.120 --> 00:26:43.123
that faction and conflict
will spread like wildfire.

00:26:44.588 --> 00:26:45.509
- And so what...

00:26:45.509 --> 00:26:47.450
And these are two fascinating threads

00:26:47.450 --> 00:26:49.460
that are probably related
to the mental health thread,

00:26:49.460 --> 00:26:51.550
what's healthy for young kids.

00:26:51.550 --> 00:26:55.270
And then what that may be
doing broadly to a generation,

00:26:55.270 --> 00:26:58.250
maybe if that's playing a
role in our polarization.

00:26:58.250 --> 00:27:00.030
There's several questions here.

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:01.150
I just saw it.

00:27:01.150 --> 00:27:02.980
There's so many questions
I'm having trouble scrolling,

00:27:02.980 --> 00:27:04.149
but there's a question about,

00:27:04.149 --> 00:27:05.740
what recommendation would you have

00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:08.079
for parents of adolescents?

00:27:08.079 --> 00:27:10.450
You take them off of social media,

00:27:10.450 --> 00:27:12.531
do you keep people to
cage so they don't care-

00:27:12.531 --> 00:27:14.070
- Yeah, so the media company-
- 'Cause they don't care?

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:16.337
- Yeah, so, the easy
thing would be to say,

00:27:16.337 --> 00:27:18.057
"Don't let your kid have social media

00:27:18.057 --> 00:27:19.620
"until they're 16 or 18."

00:27:19.620 --> 00:27:23.460
That would be an easy thing
to say, but it wouldn't work.

00:27:23.460 --> 00:27:26.000
So the first thing is to differentiate

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:28.470
between screen time and social media.

00:27:28.470 --> 00:27:31.510
So it's not screens per se that are bad.

00:27:31.510 --> 00:27:33.170
So spending time on Khan Academy,

00:27:33.170 --> 00:27:34.740
of course, you should get
some exercise during the day,

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:37.050
but spending hours learning coding

00:27:37.050 --> 00:27:38.320
is not bad for you in any way.

00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:40.070
So it's not screens,

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:42.769
it's specifically social media
where you're making content,

00:27:42.769 --> 00:27:45.167
and then you're looking,
"Who said, what about it?

00:27:45.167 --> 00:27:46.210
"What are people thinking of?"

00:27:46.210 --> 00:27:49.320
So it's specifically social media,

00:27:49.320 --> 00:27:52.260
and the effects seem biggest
on younger adolescents.

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:53.380
So my advice...

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:55.250
Okay, and then the third
thing to add in here is,

00:27:55.250 --> 00:27:58.060
we're all trapped in a game

00:27:58.060 --> 00:28:00.230
we're all trapped in a situation in which,

00:28:00.230 --> 00:28:02.270
when I told my son when
he started sixth grade,

00:28:02.270 --> 00:28:03.470
that he couldn't have Instagram,

00:28:03.470 --> 00:28:05.990
he said, "But almost everyone
else in my class has it."

00:28:05.990 --> 00:28:07.483
So it was harder for my son.

00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:10.810
Very few people wanted their
kids on, but we're all trapped.

00:28:10.810 --> 00:28:11.643
And every parent says,

00:28:11.643 --> 00:28:13.970
"Well, okay, if everyone else
is on it, I'll let you on it."

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:15.660
So I think we have to have leadership

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:17.650
from middle school principals.

00:28:17.650 --> 00:28:18.808
We have to have leadership.

00:28:18.808 --> 00:28:20.470
Parents have to get together.

00:28:20.470 --> 00:28:22.771
We have to have a common agreement

00:28:22.771 --> 00:28:24.630
to keep kids off of social media,

00:28:24.630 --> 00:28:26.010
at least until high school.

00:28:26.010 --> 00:28:28.820
I think that's the clearest thing is

00:28:28.820 --> 00:28:29.980
it should not be in middle schools.

00:28:29.980 --> 00:28:30.960
Kids should have a...

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:33.290
It's hard enough to be in middle school

00:28:33.290 --> 00:28:35.420
with all the gossip and all the changes

00:28:35.420 --> 00:28:37.650
and all the growth and
all the embarrassment.

00:28:37.650 --> 00:28:38.620
Get it out of middle school.

00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:39.868
And I think we would then see

00:28:39.868 --> 00:28:42.740
these rates of exciting depression.

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:44.613
I think they would come down somewhat.

00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:47.252
- Yeah.
- One more thing.

00:28:47.252 --> 00:28:48.447
- And when we're competition, go ahead.

00:28:48.447 --> 00:28:50.810
- Sorry, I would just
say also now for parents,

00:28:50.810 --> 00:28:52.940
I think it's also important

00:28:52.940 --> 00:28:53.890
to talk with your kids

00:28:53.890 --> 00:28:55.490
about what kind of childhood they want.

00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:56.990
Because as far as I've found,

00:28:56.990 --> 00:28:58.530
Gen Z is actually pretty reasonable,

00:28:58.530 --> 00:29:02.770
and they don't wanna spend
their whole lives online.

00:29:02.770 --> 00:29:05.160
And so helping them set limits

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:06.570
because you've got all these psychologists

00:29:06.570 --> 00:29:08.410
in Silicon Valley working

00:29:08.410 --> 00:29:10.450
so that if you give your kid
a device, they'll stay on it.

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:11.980
So this makes no sense

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:13.230
to give your child's time over

00:29:13.230 --> 00:29:15.430
to this psychologist in Silicon Valley,

00:29:15.430 --> 00:29:16.890
you and your child should decide

00:29:16.890 --> 00:29:18.930
and then use the Apple screen
time controls or whatever.

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:20.170
I think those work very well.

00:29:20.170 --> 00:29:21.330
And also agree on

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:23.520
a time that devices
come out of the bedroom.

00:29:23.520 --> 00:29:25.340
That I think is very, very important.

00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:27.462
Otherwise, kids get sucked in at night.

00:29:27.462 --> 00:29:28.820
They're doing it.

00:29:28.820 --> 00:29:30.040
It can interfere with sleep.

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:31.493
It just can go on forever.

00:29:32.560 --> 00:29:34.080
- Do you really hire psychologists

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:35.220
at social media companies-

00:29:35.220 --> 00:29:36.299
- Oh my God, yes.

00:29:36.299 --> 00:29:38.282
- To learn how to drive the
addiction factor up or the...

00:29:38.282 --> 00:29:40.590
- Absolutely, oh, my goodness, yes.

00:29:40.590 --> 00:29:42.950
I mean the psychology of slot machines,

00:29:42.950 --> 00:29:45.130
the psychology of engagement,

00:29:45.130 --> 00:29:47.100
oh yes, they hire a lot of psychologists.

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:48.530
There's a lab at Stanford

00:29:48.530 --> 00:29:53.530
that trained a lot of designers
in how do you hook people?

00:29:53.664 --> 00:29:56.660
How do you keep them on your product?

00:29:56.660 --> 00:29:58.080
Yes.
- Yeah.

00:29:58.080 --> 00:29:59.482
And then we do a little bit of work.

00:29:59.482 --> 00:30:01.210
Obviously, we think we're
doing it for a good purpose

00:30:01.210 --> 00:30:04.080
for how do you hook someone
on to learning or engagement,

00:30:04.080 --> 00:30:08.020
but we don't have a team
of cognitive scientists,

00:30:08.020 --> 00:30:08.853
but maybe you'll do it (laughs).

00:30:08.853 --> 00:30:09.910
- Try to get.

00:30:09.910 --> 00:30:13.240
Well, if your goal is to
help them finish a lesson

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:16.070
and reach a goal that
they themselves endorse,

00:30:16.070 --> 00:30:17.550
then that's just good psychology,

00:30:17.550 --> 00:30:20.870
and I'm sure you have all kinds
of points and rewards built,

00:30:20.870 --> 00:30:22.870
and that's good psychology.

00:30:22.870 --> 00:30:26.002
It's when you're trying to
undercut their own resolutions

00:30:26.002 --> 00:30:27.350
and trying to keep them on

00:30:27.350 --> 00:30:28.790
even longer than they'd like to be on.

00:30:28.790 --> 00:30:30.950
So if you look at the regret factor,

00:30:30.950 --> 00:30:31.810
how much are you...

00:30:31.810 --> 00:30:33.060
How happy are you with the time

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:35.140
that you spend on Khan Academy?

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:36.762
I would guess-
- Is that Sal?

00:30:36.762 --> 00:30:37.970
- Excuse me, hello.
- Is that Sal?

00:30:37.970 --> 00:30:39.356
- Say hello to the live audience.

00:30:39.356 --> 00:30:40.189
- Hello.

00:30:40.189 --> 00:30:42.573
- Okay.
- Hello. (laughs)

00:30:45.884 --> 00:30:50.470
- I would guess that if you ask kids,

00:30:50.470 --> 00:30:51.840
how happy are you with time?

00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:53.010
Do you wish you spend...

00:30:53.010 --> 00:30:55.040
Do you feel like you spent
too much time on Khan Academy?

00:30:55.040 --> 00:30:56.330
Well, okay, if the
parents were forcing them,

00:30:56.330 --> 00:30:57.340
maybe they'll say it's too much,

00:30:57.340 --> 00:31:00.540
but if it's self-driven,
they'd be happy with it.

00:31:00.540 --> 00:31:02.202
- Yeah, no, I mean these conversations,

00:31:02.202 --> 00:31:04.170
we could talk for several hours,

00:31:04.170 --> 00:31:05.720
and hopefully, we can have you back on

00:31:05.720 --> 00:31:07.460
'cause each of these threads,

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:10.600
I think we can double and
triple and quadruple click on,

00:31:10.600 --> 00:31:14.570
maybe just to kind of finish
up maybe on a positive note,

00:31:14.570 --> 00:31:15.980
I know you're above and beyond

00:31:15.980 --> 00:31:17.760
your academic research and your writing

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:21.990
you're involved in trying to change

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:23.960
maybe at least not only observed,

00:31:23.960 --> 00:31:25.763
but maybe deep polarized society.

00:31:27.453 --> 00:31:28.870
We have this question from
Facebook, Cathy Gifford Grans,

00:31:28.870 --> 00:31:31.230
what positive actions can individuals take

00:31:31.230 --> 00:31:33.280
to mitigate some of the negative impact

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:34.500
of increasing polarization?

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:38.712
So, what should we do
collectively or individually?

00:31:38.712 --> 00:31:39.545
- Thank you.

00:31:39.545 --> 00:31:42.500
Thank you, Cathy, for that question.

00:31:42.500 --> 00:31:45.650
So a lot of the big changes
are gonna be systemic changes

00:31:45.650 --> 00:31:49.560
in elections and media
and all sorts of things.

00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:51.770
But all of us as individuals,

00:31:51.770 --> 00:31:54.640
all of us are caught up in a system

00:31:54.640 --> 00:31:56.911
that makes us hyper moralize.

00:31:56.911 --> 00:32:00.002
And so to go back to the
happiness hypothesis,

00:32:00.002 --> 00:32:01.760
one of the great truths

00:32:01.760 --> 00:32:03.830
is that we're too judgmental.

00:32:03.830 --> 00:32:04.663
Is that...

00:32:04.663 --> 00:32:08.603
Here let me see, I actually
have some quotes here.

00:32:10.530 --> 00:32:11.600
Let's see.

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:13.697
So this is something
you hear from all over,

00:32:13.697 --> 00:32:15.861
"Judge not lest ye be judged."

00:32:15.861 --> 00:32:19.157
Buddhists says, "Hatred
never ends through hatred

00:32:19.157 --> 00:32:21.338
"by love alone; does it end."

00:32:21.338 --> 00:32:24.937
Sengstan says, "When love
and hate are both absent,

00:32:24.937 --> 00:32:26.767
"everything becomes clear and undisguised.

00:32:26.767 --> 00:32:29.062
"Make the smallest distinction, however,

00:32:29.062 --> 00:32:30.060
"and heaven and earth are
set infinitely apart."

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:31.860
We are so judgmental,

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:34.470
and we also have the capacity
to understand people.

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:35.350
And when we do that,

00:32:35.350 --> 00:32:38.370
we lose some of our anger,
and we can talk to them.

00:32:38.370 --> 00:32:40.493
So what I would recomend
is everybody realize

00:32:40.493 --> 00:32:44.203
we're caught up in endless
streams of outrage,

00:32:44.203 --> 00:32:48.720
and you as an individual
can step out of it.

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:50.220
Can reach out to people,

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:51.870
can try to understand them.

00:32:51.870 --> 00:32:53.870
I recommend, especially the book

00:32:53.870 --> 00:32:56.550
How to Win Friends and Influence
People, by Dale Carnegie.

00:32:56.550 --> 00:32:59.960
It'll give you almost a social superpower.

00:32:59.960 --> 00:33:03.813
You have the capacity to
actually persuade people

00:33:03.813 --> 00:33:05.990
to actually connect with people.

00:33:05.990 --> 00:33:10.010
And social media pulls us
into this conflict mindset,

00:33:10.010 --> 00:33:11.662
but you'll be much more
successful in life.

00:33:11.662 --> 00:33:15.180
If you try to understand
people, persuade them,

00:33:15.180 --> 00:33:17.330
and learn to work with them.

00:33:17.330 --> 00:33:19.153
At very least it'll help
you in your own marriage

00:33:19.153 --> 00:33:20.503
and with your own children.

00:33:21.760 --> 00:33:23.920
- And related to that, I
know we're already over time,

00:33:23.920 --> 00:33:24.950
but there's another really good question.

00:33:24.950 --> 00:33:26.287
It also will help tie a bow on this.

00:33:26.287 --> 00:33:30.190
There's a question from
Facebook, John Hepworth,

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:32.390
is there a way to detoxify social media?

00:33:32.390 --> 00:33:33.500
You're having these debates.

00:33:33.500 --> 00:33:35.060
I know Facebook is struggling.

00:33:35.060 --> 00:33:37.890
Do you take off certain posts?

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:39.800
And even if they're from
the president or not,

00:33:39.800 --> 00:33:42.040
and there's really kind
of a no-win situation,

00:33:42.040 --> 00:33:43.720
you're gonna make someone upset,

00:33:43.720 --> 00:33:44.980
and then there's free speech rights,

00:33:44.980 --> 00:33:47.950
but then there's the page,
there's all these things.

00:33:47.950 --> 00:33:50.523
What would you do if you
were Mark Zuckerberg,

00:33:50.523 --> 00:33:52.140
would you edit speech?

00:33:52.140 --> 00:33:53.320
Would you change the platform

00:33:53.320 --> 00:33:56.044
in some way to make it less
toxic or to detoxify it?

00:33:56.044 --> 00:33:58.180
- Yes, I think it's essential

00:33:58.180 --> 00:34:00.050
that we make the platforms less toxic.

00:34:00.050 --> 00:34:03.440
There was a report in the Wall
Street Journal a month ago

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:05.262
about how Facebook had.

00:34:05.262 --> 00:34:07.960
They had studied polarization.

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:09.730
They had thought about ways to reduce

00:34:09.730 --> 00:34:12.447
the degree to which it
exacerbates polarization,

00:34:12.447 --> 00:34:13.570
and they dropped it.

00:34:13.570 --> 00:34:14.403
So I think Facebook,

00:34:14.403 --> 00:34:15.870
there's a lot of responsibility here.

00:34:15.870 --> 00:34:17.300
There's a lot more that
they could be doing.

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:20.120
Twitter has hardly any social
psychologist on its staff.

00:34:20.120 --> 00:34:22.740
I think Twitter needs to really
start studying these issues.

00:34:22.740 --> 00:34:24.920
They've taken a couple
of steps here and there.

00:34:24.920 --> 00:34:26.000
I have an article in the Atlantic

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:29.880
with Tobias Rose Stockwell last November,

00:34:29.880 --> 00:34:31.692
in which we laid out a few
steps that could be done.

00:34:31.692 --> 00:34:34.870
One is to add friction here and there

00:34:34.870 --> 00:34:36.060
when you're about to post something

00:34:36.060 --> 00:34:38.060
that seems to be particularly
aggressive or toxic.

00:34:38.060 --> 00:34:39.310
You can add little bits of friction,

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:40.840
but that's a small thing.

00:34:40.840 --> 00:34:42.317
More algorithm accountability.

00:34:42.317 --> 00:34:43.350
And the algorithm...

00:34:43.350 --> 00:34:45.110
Most people are pretty reasonable.

00:34:45.110 --> 00:34:46.190
This is wherever I go.

00:34:46.190 --> 00:34:47.310
Most people are pretty reasonable,

00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:50.350
but the algorithms give much
more voice to the people

00:34:50.350 --> 00:34:51.960
at the extremes that
people are not reasonable

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:53.280
'cause they generate engagement.

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:54.810
We need to understand those algorithms,

00:34:54.810 --> 00:34:57.240
and there needs to be
some public comment on it,

00:34:57.240 --> 00:34:58.712
some way to change the algorithms.

00:34:58.712 --> 00:35:02.690
I think the biggest bang is
gonna come when we find ways

00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:05.410
not to have to go after
each individual post

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:09.740
but to reward users who are
civil and show some nuance

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:11.350
and some constructiveness.

00:35:11.350 --> 00:35:15.280
And so, if I could mark people

00:35:15.280 --> 00:35:18.340
who I think are particularly
or not me marking.

00:35:18.340 --> 00:35:22.990
If there were ways to code
for integrative complexity

00:35:22.990 --> 00:35:25.310
or civility or constructiveness,

00:35:25.310 --> 00:35:27.160
if the platforms could code for things

00:35:27.160 --> 00:35:28.297
so that I could set, "You know what,

00:35:28.297 --> 00:35:29.877
"I don't want anybody
who scored score zero.

00:35:29.877 --> 00:35:30.797
"I don't want trolls

00:35:30.797 --> 00:35:33.141
"who just their content
is very predictable.

00:35:33.141 --> 00:35:35.187
"I want to cut them out
so that they don't see me

00:35:35.187 --> 00:35:36.270
"I don't see them."

00:35:36.270 --> 00:35:40.693
We would all have a much, much
better life on social media.

00:35:41.840 --> 00:35:43.830
So I think this is one of the trends.

00:35:43.830 --> 00:35:46.770
If this keeps going on, I
think our public square,

00:35:46.770 --> 00:35:49.470
our ability to talk to each
other is in big trouble.

00:35:49.470 --> 00:35:51.370
I think we're gonna have
to have major reforms

00:35:51.370 --> 00:35:53.513
to social media that makes it less nasty

00:35:53.513 --> 00:35:58.093
less conspiracy generating,
less outrage generating.

00:35:58.970 --> 00:36:00.510
- Hey, one question.

00:36:00.510 --> 00:36:02.260
And I know I keep asking
questions over time,

00:36:02.260 --> 00:36:04.750
but I mean, it sounds
like some of these reforms

00:36:04.750 --> 00:36:07.440
would just be bad business for these comp

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:08.534
'cause obviously, it's oftentimes

00:36:08.534 --> 00:36:09.990
the more extreme statements

00:36:09.990 --> 00:36:11.360
the things that are generating outrage

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:12.270
that get you sucked in,

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:13.340
that make you wanna comment,

00:36:13.340 --> 00:36:14.630
that make you click on something.

00:36:14.630 --> 00:36:16.410
And it's not the social media,

00:36:16.410 --> 00:36:19.544
as you mentioned, our
mainstream media or TV media,

00:36:19.544 --> 00:36:22.230
or print media-
- I think of it a lot too.

00:36:22.230 --> 00:36:25.283
- They've also discovered
this business model.

00:36:25.283 --> 00:36:26.640
- That's right, yes.

00:36:26.640 --> 00:36:27.790
As many have commented,

00:36:27.790 --> 00:36:30.230
the business model is such
that we are not the customers,

00:36:30.230 --> 00:36:32.390
it's the advertisers
who are the customers,

00:36:32.390 --> 00:36:33.852
where the tuna fish that
gets hooked on the hook

00:36:33.852 --> 00:36:37.013
and delivered to the customers in a sense.

00:36:38.800 --> 00:36:41.993
So yes, the business model
will have to change ultimately,

00:36:43.010 --> 00:36:43.990
but I would put it this way.

00:36:43.990 --> 00:36:45.690
So I teach a course at NYU Stern

00:36:45.690 --> 00:36:47.090
on professional responsibility.

00:36:47.090 --> 00:36:48.260
It's our ethics course.

00:36:48.260 --> 00:36:49.420
And one of the things that becomes clear

00:36:49.420 --> 00:36:50.950
when you look at all these cases

00:36:50.950 --> 00:36:54.810
is that in the short
run, cheating often pays,

00:36:54.810 --> 00:36:57.690
but in the long run,
ethics generally does pay.

00:36:57.690 --> 00:36:59.920
And so, in the long run,

00:36:59.920 --> 00:37:02.394
if people really hate
Facebook and the more...

00:37:02.394 --> 00:37:04.375
We're seeing that this month,

00:37:04.375 --> 00:37:07.890
boycotts can be extremely costly.

00:37:07.890 --> 00:37:10.120
And if there's all kinds of
people wanna boycott something,

00:37:10.120 --> 00:37:12.920
well, if someone comes
up with a better product

00:37:12.920 --> 00:37:14.220
that is not as tox...

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:16.020
That does not lead to such toxicity.

00:37:18.050 --> 00:37:19.583
Facebook would lose market share.

00:37:19.583 --> 00:37:23.750
So, in the long run, I think ethics pays.

00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:27.970
And also, most of the people
I met in Silicon Valley

00:37:27.970 --> 00:37:29.460
it's full of idealistic people

00:37:29.460 --> 00:37:31.970
who really think or want to believe

00:37:31.970 --> 00:37:33.660
that they're making the world better.

00:37:33.660 --> 00:37:36.260
So I am hopeful that some
big changes will happen,

00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:38.063
but big changes have to happen.

00:37:39.550 --> 00:37:40.720
- Yeah, well, Jonathan,

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:42.211
you've given us so much to think about.

00:37:42.211 --> 00:37:44.460
I encourage everyone to take a look at

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:45.780
all of Jonathan's writing.

00:37:45.780 --> 00:37:48.072
You said, the coddling.com.

00:37:48.072 --> 00:37:50.450
- You could go to the coddling.com.

00:37:50.450 --> 00:37:52.248
Yeah, you can get
everywhere else from there.

00:37:52.248 --> 00:37:54.230
- To get some more data.

00:37:54.230 --> 00:37:55.540
But, Jonathan, thank you so much.

00:37:55.540 --> 00:37:57.470
This was extremely insightful.

00:37:57.470 --> 00:37:59.230
And you've given me a lot to think about.

00:37:59.230 --> 00:38:00.810
- Great, my pleasure, Sal.

00:38:00.810 --> 00:38:02.400
Thanks for all that you and your team do

00:38:02.400 --> 00:38:03.533
to educate the world.

00:38:04.840 --> 00:38:07.080
